 \t_{2b}, \; \z \rangle
 \end{split}
\end{equation}
are both normal in $G$, and that there are two short exact sequences
\begin{equation} \label{eq:K1K2}
 \begin{split}
 &1 \to K_1 \to G \to Q_2 \to 1 \\
 &1 \to K_2 \to G \to Q_1 \to 1,
 \end{split}
\end{equation}
such that the elements $\r_{21}, \, \t_{21}, \ldots, \r_{2b}, \, \t_{2b}$
yield a complete system of coset representatives for $Q_2$, whereas the
elements
$\r_{11}, \, \t_{11}, \ldots, \r_{1b}, \, \t_{1b}$ yield a complete system
of coset
representatives for $Q_1$. We will say that a diagonal double Kodaira structure on $G$ is called \emph{strong} if $K_1=K_2=G$.

expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, the authors “combine techniques from Combinatorial Group Theory with explicit computations carried out using the computer algebra system \verb|GAP4|”. Using that “$\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b)$ admits a finite geometric presentation with $4b+1$ generators” (Gonz\'alez-Meneses and Guaschi, cited as \cite[Theorem 7]{GG04}), they apply an admissible epimorphism to obtain an ordered generating tuple
\[
\mathfrak{S}=(\r_{11},\,\t_{11},\ldots,\r_{2b},\,\t_{2b},\,\mathsf{z}),
\]
where “$\mathsf{z}$ … is a commutator satisfying $o(\mathsf{z})=n$”, and the tuple satisfies “a system of relations induced by those of $\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b)$.” Such a tuple is called a “\emph{diagonal double Kodaira structure} of type $(b,n)$ on $G$”, so the existence of an admissible epimorphism is “reformulated as the algebraic-combinatorial problem of determining a diagonal double Kodaira structure of type $(b,n)$ on $G$.”

They then reduce the search space using structural results developed later. We first record the following remark.
\begin{remark}\label{rmk:cct-no-prestructure}
A CCT-group does not admit any prestructure, see \cite[Proposition 4.4]{PolSab22}. Consequently, by Remark \ref{remark:prestructure-structure}, the search for diagonal double Kodaira structures can be restricted to non-CCT groups.

\end{remark}

Next they use the following proposition.
\begin{proposition} \label{prop:monolithic-argument} If $G$ is a finite group, then the following holds.
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\boldsymbol{(1)}$ ] Assume that  $G$ admits a prestructure  $\mathfrak{P}$ of type $n$.  Then either $G$ is monolithic, and $\z \in \operatorname{mon}(G)$, or  there exist a proper quotient $H$ of $G$ and a prestructure $\bar{\P}$ of type $\bar{n}$ on $H$, with $\bar{n}$ dividing $n$, such that $\P$ is obtained as a lifting of $\bar{\P}$ via the quotient homomorphism $\pi \colon G \to H$.
\item[$\boldsymbol{(2)}$ ] Assume that  $G$ admits a double Kodaira structure  $\mathfrak{S}$ of type $(b, \, n)$.  Then either $G$ is monolithic, and $\z \in \operatorname{mon}(G)$, or  there exist a proper quotient $H$ of $G$ and a prestructure $\bar{\S}$ of type $(b, \, \bar{n})$ on $H$, with $\bar{n}$ dividing $n$, such that $\S$ is obtained as a lifting of $\bar{\S}$ via the quotient homomorphism $\pi \colon G \to H$.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}
Thus one can exclude “non-monolithic groups whose proper quotients do not admit diagonal double Kodaira structures.” They also use the following sufficient condition.
\begin{corollary} \label{cor:prestructure-b-3}
Let $G$ be a finite group such that, for every prestructure $\P$ in $G$, the subgroup $K=\langle \r_{11}, \, \t_{11}, \, \r_{12}, \, \t_{12} \rangle$ satisfies $C_G(K)=Z(G)$. Then every diagonal double Kodaira structure on $G$ $($if any$)$ is necessarily of type $(2, \, n)$. Equivalently, any admissible epimorphism onto $G$ $($if it exists$)$ must be of the form $\varphi \colon \mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_2) \to G$.
\end{corollary}

After excluding these cases, “the remaining groups of order $|G|\le 127$ are then examined individually … with the help of our \verb|GAP4| routine \verb|CheckStructure| … leading to the proof of the main theorem,” i.e. by an explicit computational verification/classification among the remaining small groups.

