\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|x|^\alpha |w_{k+1}|^q\,dx \leq C\|w_{k+1}\|_{s, p, \beta}^{\frac{pe_2+qe_1}{e_1+e_2}}\leq C_{k+1}\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|x|^\alpha |w_{k}|^q\,dx \right)^{\frac{pe_2+qe_1}{pe_1+pe_2}}= C_{k+1}\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|x|^\alpha |w_{k}|^q\,dx \right)^{1+\delta}
\end{split}
\end{equation}for some $\delta>0$ since $q>p$. Therefore, by the numerical lemma \cite[Lemma 13]{FSV}, we obtain that there is $\varepsilon\in (0, 1)$ such that if
 \begin{equation}\label{assumption with eps}
 \int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|x|^\alpha |w_{0}|^q\,dx <\varepsilon
 \end{equation}then
 $$\lim_{k\to \infty}\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|x|^\alpha |w_{k}|^q\,dx =0.$$Hence, \eqref{convergence to u} implies $\|u\|_{L^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^N)}\leq 1$. Observe that 
 $$\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|x|^\alpha |w_{0}|^q\,dx=\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} |x|^\alpha u_{+}^q\,dx$$so the result is valid under the assumption
 $$\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} u_{+}^q|x|^\alpha\,dx<\varepsilon.$$If now
 $\xi=\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} u_{+}^q|x|^\alpha\,dx>0$ is arbitrary, then we choose $C>1$ large enough so that
 \begin{equation}
\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} \left(\frac{u_{+}}{C}\right)^q|x|^\alpha\,dx<\varepsilon.
 \end{equation}Moreover, $u/C$ also satisfies \eqref{estimate w k} with an extra constant at the end. Hence, from the above argument 
