\label{one rel quotients}

Let $\Gamma$ be a (finitely presented) infinite group with property $\T$, that is hyperbolic relative to a subgroup $K$. Then there exists a (finitely presented) quotient $\pi \colon \Gamma \to \bar{\Gamma}$ that has finite kernel when restricted to $K$, such that $\bar{\Gamma}$ is hyperbolic relative to $\pi(K)$, and does not have property $\TT$. In fact, $\HHH^2(\bar{\Gamma}; V) \neq 0$ for every unitary $\bar{\Gamma}$-representation $V$.
\end{propA}

