The post-theorem introduction does not give a step-by-step proof of the main theorem, but it does indicate the main mechanism and key analytic input:

- The stabilization/global boundedness in the main theorem is attributed to enhanced dissipation: “The stabilizing phenomenon is fundamentally caused by the enhanced dissipation induced by the Taylor-Couette flow.”

- A “crucial role” is played by a recalled space-time estimate (Proposition~\ref{prop:space time}, from Proposition 6.1 in \cite{AHL2024}) for the linearized/Fourier-mode system
\begin{equation}\label{eq:space time}
		\left\{
		\begin{array}{lr}
			\partial_{t}h-\frac{1}{A}\Big(\partial_{r}^{2}-\frac{k^{2}-\frac14}{r^{2}} \Big)h+\frac{ik}{r^{2}}h+\frac{1}{r}\big[ikh_{1}-r^{\frac12}\partial_{r}(r^{\frac12}h_{2}) \big]=0,\\
			h|_{t=0}=h(0),\quad h|_{r=1,R}=0,
		\end{array}
		\right.
	\end{equation}
which yields weighted bounds of the form
  \[
  \|e^{aA^{-\frac13}|k|^{\frac23}R^{-2}t}h\|_{L^\infty L^2}+\cdots \le C\big(\|h(0)\|_{L^2}+A^{\frac16}|k|^{-\frac13}\|e^{aA^{-\frac13}|k|^{\frac23}R^{-2}t}kh_1\|_{L^2L^2}+A^{\frac12}\|e^{\cdots}h_2\|_{L^2L^2}\big).
  \]
This estimate is described as the tool that “plays a crucial role in the subsequent analysis.”

- A main technical difficulty in proving the main theorem is identified in controlling the “$T_1$ term” in the estimates of $n$:
  \[
  \|n\|_{Y_a}\le C\Big(\sum_{k\ne0}\|n_k(0)\|_{L^2}+\underbrace{\sum_{k\ne0}A^{\frac16}|k|^{-\frac13}\|e^{aA^{-\frac13}|k|^{\frac23}R^{-2}t}k f_1\|_{L^2L^2}}_{=:T_1}+\cdots\Big),
  \]
  with
  \[
  \|f_1\|_{L^2}\le \frac{C(R)}{A}\Big(\sum_{l\in\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0,k\}}|l|^{-\frac12}\|w_l\|_{L^2}\|n_{k-l}\|_{L^2}+\cdots\Big).
  \]
  They state: “We estimate the norm by considering the characteristics of each of the four cases based on frequency.”

- The theorem’s global boundedness is emphasized as coming “solely by the strength of the Taylor-Couette flow (i.e., a sufficiently large $A$),” i.e. “no smallness assumption on the initial cell mass or on the initial velocity field.”

- Finally, the paper roadmap says that “some key ideas and the proof of the main theorem are presented” in
\begin{aligned}
				&	\|n(t)\|_{L^{1}}\leq\|n_{\rm in}\|_{L^{1}}=:M.
			\end{aligned}
		\end{equation*}

		\item Throughout this paper, we denote by $ C $ a positive constant independent of $A$, $t$ and the initial data, and it may be different from line to line. $C(R)$  denotes a constant depending on the parameter $R$.
	\end{itemize}

	The rest part of this paper is organized as follows. In Section \ref{ideas}, some key ideas and the proof of
