The proof strategy of Theorem \ref{thm:main} follows the framework proposed in \cite{CGT24}. The key step is the derivation of a Fluctuation-Dissipation relation:

\begin{theorem}[The Fluctuation-Dissipation Theorem]\label{thm:F-D}
	Let the divergence-free functions $\varphi,\,\psi\in\fS(\TT^d,\RR^d)$ be fixed, and define 
	\begin{align}\label{eq:fi}
		f_1:=\varphi,\quad f_2:=[\psi\otimes\varphi]_{sym}=\frac12(\varphi\otimes\psi+\psi\otimes\varphi)\,.
	\end{align}
	Then for $i=2,3$, there exists a family $\mathcal V^i=\{v^{N,n}\in\oplus_{j=i}^n\FK_j, n\in\NN\}$ and a positive constant $D$ (which is independent of $i$) such that
	\begin{align}
		&\lim_{n\to\infty}\limsup_{N\to\infty}\|v^{N,n}\|=0,\label{5.1}\\
		&\lim_{n\to\infty}\limsup_{N\to\infty}\|(-\fL_0)^{-\frac12}(-\fL^Nv^{N,n}-\fA^N_+f_{i-1}+\fA_-^Nv_i^{N,n})\|=0,\label{5.2}\\
		&\lim_{n\to\infty}\limsup_{N\to\infty}\|(-\fL_0)^{-\frac12}(\fA_-^Nv_i^{N,n}-D\fL_0 f_{i-1})\|=0\,.\label{5.3}
	\end{align}
\end{theorem}

This theorem approximates the non-linearity term $-\lambda_N\int_0^tB^{N}(u^{N}_s)(\varphi)ds$ in the identity
\begin{equation}\label{3.1}
     u^{N}_t(\varphi) = u_0^{N}(\varphi) + \int_0^t u^{N}_s(\Delta\varphi)ds - \lambda_N\int_0^tB^{N}(u^{N}_s)(\varphi)ds + M_t^{\varphi},
\end{equation}
by a drift $\int_0^tD\fL_0^Nu_s^N(\varphi)\operatorname{d}\!  s$, capturing the additional diffusivity, along with a Dynkin martingale that represents the extra noise.

To obtain the martingale term, they truncate the generator $\mathcal{L}^N$ of $u^N$, and then it can be obtained by solving the truncated resolvent equation $-\mathcal{L}^N_{\geq 2}v^N = \mathcal{A}^N_+\varphi\,.$

In the critical case $d=2$, they approximate the resolvent solution by a sequence of diagonal symmetric operators through a Replacement Lemma, an approach originating from \cite{CET23b} and simplified in \cite{CG24,CGT24}.

In the supercritical case $d\geq3$, they follow the routine in \cite{CGT24} to analyze $v^N$, but note new issues: since $u^N$ is vector-valued and divergence-free, the presence of the Leray projection in the nonlinearity makes all vector components of $u^N$ be coupled. To address this they perform a case-by-case analysis depending on the action of the Leray projection, and use the anti-symmetric part of the generator, along with a rotational change of coordinates in the integral, to derive the desired decoupled limiting stochastic heat equation from the original coupled system
\begin{equation}\label{eq:scaled-NS}
    \partial_t u^N = \Delta u^N -\lambda_N \rho^N * \Lp \operatorname{div}((\rho^N*u^N)\otimes (\rho^N*u^N)) + \sqrt{2}(-\Delta)^{\frac{1}{2}}\Lp\xi\,,\quad \nabla \cdot u^N = 0\,,
\end{equation}
which is the main step in establishing the main theorem.

They also give an asymptotic expansion for $D$ in $d\geq3$ by induction argument and variational formula, and emphasize that in $d\geq3$ both the diagonal and off terms of $\langle(-\fL_0)^{-1}\fA_+^N\psi,\fA_+^N\psi\rangle$ for $\psi\in\Gamma L^2$ could produce extra terms as $N\to\infty$, which cannot be approximated by a replacement diagonal operator.

