Estimating the operator norm (denoted by $\left\lVert \cdot \rv_{op}$) is one of the classic problems in random matrix theory. If the random matrix is 'highly' symmetric (e.g. the entries are i.i.d) then this question is well understood cf. \cite{mac:los}. The issue becomes more complicated when we are dealing with an inhomogeneous case. For example we consider $(X_{ij})_{i,j \leq n}$ i.i.d. random variables and we want to upper bound

\begin{theorem}\label{prop:oszapodgaus}
Let $(a_{ij})_{i,j\leq n}$ be the matrix such that $a_{ii}=0$. Let $T\subset \eR^{n^2}$. Assume that $(X_{ij})_{i,j\leq n}$ 
 are independent, symmetric, $\eta$-subgaussian random variables. Then, we have
\begin{align}
 &\Ex \sup_{t\in T} \lv (a_{ij} X_{ij} t_{ij})_{i,j \leq n} \rv_{op} \nonumber \\
 & \lesssim^\eta \Log^{3/2}(d_A) \left(\sup_{v,w \in B_2}\Ex \sup_{t\in T}\sum_{ij}a_{ij} X_{ij} t_{ij}v_iw_j +\RTX\right.\nonumber \\
 &+\left.\MTI +\MTJ+\Ex \sup_{t\in T} \max_{ij} |a_{ij}t_{ij} X_{ij}|\right). \label{eq:loctw1}
