We first introduce some notations before decomposing in physical space.
\begin{definition} \cite[Definition 3.4]{GW} \label{dual-box}
Let $U\subset \R^m$ be a box of dimensions $a_1\times \dots\times a_m$. Define $U^*$ to be the {\bf dual box} of $U$ centered at the origin of dimensions $a_1^{-1}\times \dots\times a_m^{-1}$, and with edges parallel to the corresponding edges of $U$.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition} \cite[Remark 3.6]{GW} \label{dilation}
For a slab $U\subset \R^{n-1}$ of dimensions $Rs^2\times Rs\times \dots\times Rs$ with $s\in[R^{-1/2},1]$ and a constant $C>0$, define $CU$, the non-isotropic $C$-dilation of $U$, to be a slab of dimensions $C^2Rs^2\times CRs\times \dots\times CRs$ with the same center as $U$.

For two slabs $U_1, U_2\subset \R^n$, we say they are \textbf{comparable} if 
    \[\frac{1}{C}U_1\subset U_2\subset C U_1.\] 
Here, $C=C_\phi$ is a constant that may vary from line to line, but eventually only depends on $\phi$. 

\end{definition}

We now carry out the wave packet decomposition of $f$. Fix $\theta \in \Theta_{R^{-1/2}}$.
We cover $\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$ by a finitely overlapping family of
