\subsection{Notations} We write $\#E$ for the cardinality of a finite set $E$. If $\mathcal{E}$ is a family of sets in $\mathbb{R}^n$, we use $\cup \mathcal{E}$ to denote $\bigcup_{E \in \mathcal{E}} E$. For $A,B \ge 0$, we write $A \lesssim B$ to mean $A \le C B$ for some absolute constant $C$, and $A \sim B$ to mean both $A \lesssim B$ and $B \lesssim A$. For $R>1$, we write $A \lesssim_\eta B$ to indicate that $A \le C_\eta R^\eta B$ for all $\eta>0$. We use $\mathrm{RapDec}(R)$ to denote a rapidly decaying quantity, meaning that for all $R \ge 1$,
\[
\mathrm{RapDec}(R) \le C_N R^{-N} \qquad \textrm{for some very large $N$} .
\]

Finally, for a set $X$, we write $N_r(X)$ for its $r$-neighborhood.
