post_theorem_intro_text:
We briefly explain the difficulties and techniques in the proof. As in the study of $\Phi_{|5K_X|}$ in \cite{Tod07, LDi12, Chen12, CD25}, the basic idea is to creat non-klt centers by constructing a boundary $\Delta\sim_{{\mathbb Q}}\delta K_X$ for some $\delta<2$
and cut down the dimension of non-klt centers by the Anghern--Siu type method.
The issue in dealing with $\Phi_{|2K_X|}$ instead of $\Phi_{|5K_X|}$ or $\Phi_{|3K_X|}$ is that, often we are not allowed to cut down the dimension of a non-klt center if the canonical volume of the non-klt center
is small (e.g., a surface with small canonical volume or a curve with geometric genus $2$), otherwise the constant $\delta$ will exceed $2$. In this case, instead of cutting down the dimension of the non-klt center, we prove certain extension theorems which allow us to lift global sections from the centers to $X$. 
Here we remark that in \cite{CL24}, the key step is to prove an extension theorem for fibrations, where many techniques such as the Kawamata--Viehweg vanishing theorem, canonical bundle formula, the Zariski--Nakayama decomposition, the Hacon--M\textsuperscript{c}Kernan extension theorem, and Birkar's lower bound of lct are involved, and the extension theorem is not effective; in this paper, we prove an effective version of extension theorem by using the Nadel vanishing theorem and for curve centers we do not require them to form a fibration. 

As a bi-product of our method, we prove the following theorem concerning the pluricanonical maps of $3$-folds of general type fibered by $(1,2)$-surfaces or $(2,3)$-surfaces, as a complement of the results of Xu \cite[Theorem~1.4, Theorem~1.5]{XuJ14}:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm1.3}
Let $X$ be a smooth $3$-fold of general type. Suppose that $X$ admits a fibration $X\to C$ to a curve with a general fiber denoted by $F$.
\begin{enumerate}
 \item If $F$ is a $(1,2)$-surface and $\Vol(X)> 60$, then $\Phi_{|mK_X|}$ is generically finite of degree $2$ for $2\leq m \leq 4$ and is birational for $m\geq 5$.
 \item If $F$ is a $(2,3)$-surface and $\Vol(X)>456$, then $\Phi_{|mK_X|}$ is generically finite of degree $2$ for $2\leq m \leq 3$ and is birational for $m\geq 4$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

ref_dict.rmk:cct-no-prestructure:
\begin{remark}\label{rmk:cct-no-prestructure}
A CCT-group does not admit any prestructure, see \cite[Proposition 4.4]{PolSab22}. Consequently, by Remark \ref{remark:prestructure-structure}, the search for diagonal double Kodaira structures can be restricted to non-CCT groups.

\end{remark}

ref_dict.cor:prestructure-b-3:
\begin{corollary} \label{cor:prestructure-b-3}
Let $G$ be a finite group such that, for every prestructure $\P$ in $G$, the subgroup $K=\langle \r_{11}, \, \t_{11}, \, \r_{12}, \, \t_{12} \rangle$ satisfies $C_G(K)=Z(G)$. Then every diagonal double Kodaira structure on $G$ $($if any$)$ is necessarily of type $(2, \, n)$. Equivalently, any admissible epimorphism onto $G$ $($if it exists$)$ must be of the form $\varphi \colon \mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_2) \to G$.
\end{corollary}

ref_dict.prop:monolithic-argument:
\begin{proposition} \label{prop:monolithic-argument} If $G$ is a finite group, then the following holds.
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\boldsymbol{(1)}$ ] Assume that  $G$ admits a prestructure  $\mathfrak{P}$ of type $n$.  Then either $G$ is monolithic, and $\z \in \operatorname{mon}(G)$, or  there exist a proper quotient $H$ of $G$ and a prestructure $\bar{\P}$ of type $\bar{n}$ on $H$, with $\bar{n}$ dividing $n$, such that $\P$ is obtained as a lifting of $\bar{\P}$ via the quotient homomorphism $\pi \colon G \to H$.
\item[$\boldsymbol{(2)}$ ] Assume that  $G$ admits a double Kodaira structure  $\mathfrak{S}$ of type $(b, \, n)$.  Then either $G$ is monolithic, and $\z \in \operatorname{mon}(G)$, or  there exist a proper quotient $H$ of $G$ and a prestructure $\bar{\S}$ of type $(b, \, \bar{n})$ on $H$, with $\bar{n}$ dividing $n$, such that $\S$ is obtained as a lifting of $\bar{\S}$ via the quotient homomorphism $\pi \colon G \to H$.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}

ref_dict.cor:b=3_128:
\begin{corollary} \label{cor:b=3_128}
If $b \ge 3$, then every admissible quotient of $\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b)$ has order at least $128$.
\end{corollary}

pre_theorem_intro_text:
\label{sec:intro}
Let $\Sigma_b$ be a compact Riemann surface of genus $b$. We write $\mathsf{P}_k(\Sigma_b)$ for the pure braid group on $k$ strands of $\Sigma_b$, defined as the fundamental group $\pi_1(\operatorname{Conf}_k \Sigma_b)$ of the configuration space
\begin{equation} \label{eq:conf-space}
\operatorname{Conf}_k \Sigma_b = \left\{(x_1, \ldots, x_k) \in (\Sigma_b)^k \; | \; x_i \neq x_j  \; \operatorname{for} \;i \neq j \right \}.
\end{equation}
For every choice of $b$ and $k$, surface braid groups are finitely presented, and several presentations appear in the literature (e.g. \cite{Bir69, Scott70, Bel04, GG04}). These groups play a relevant role in low-dimensional topology and related fields, bridging ideas from algebraic topology, geometric topology, and algebraic geometry.

A series of recent works \cite{CaPol19, Pol22, PolSab22, PolSab23, PolSab24} has clarified how certain finite quotients of $\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b) = \pi_1(\Sigma_b \times \Sigma_b - \Delta)$, where $\Delta \subset \Sigma_b \times \Sigma_b$ is the diagonal, relate to the theory of double Kodaira fibred surfaces. More precisely, let $A_{12}$ denote the homotopy class of the geometric braid that loops once around the diagonal. A surjective homomorphism 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:admissible-epi}   
\varphi \colon \mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b) \longrightarrow G
\end{equation}
onto a finite group $G$, such that $\varphi(A_{12})$ has order $n \geq 2$, will be called an \emph{admissible epimorphism} of  $\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b)$. We will also say that the finite group $G$ is an \emph{admissible} (\emph{pure}) \emph{braid quotient of tipe} $(b, \, n)$. 

Since $n \geq 2$, a surjective homomorphism as in \eqref{eq:admissible-epi} is admissible if and only if it does not factor through $\pi_1(\Sigma_b \times \Sigma_b)$. Moreover, being $A_{12}$ a commutator in $\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b)$, an admissible braid quotient $G$ is necessarily \emph{non-abelian}, see Remark \ref{rmk:purebraidquotient_nonabelian}.

By Grauert-Remmert's extension theorem and Serre's GAGA, an admissible braid quotient as in \eqref{eq:admissible-epi} determines a Galois cover
\begin{equation} \label{eq:cover-from-admissible-epi}   
\mathbf{f} \colon S \longrightarrow \Sigma_b \times \Sigma_b,
\end{equation} 
where $S$ is a smooth projective surface. The Galois group of this cover is $G$ and its branch locus is exactly $\Delta$, with branching index equal to $n$. 

Composing the  map $\mathbf{f} \colon S \longrightarrow \Sigma_b \times \Sigma_b$ with the two projections onto the natural factors of $\Sigma_b \times \Sigma_b$, and performing a simultaneous Stein factorization if necessary, we get to smooth fibrations 
\begin{equation}
f_i \colon S \longrightarrow  \Sigma_b, \quad i=1, \, 2,
\end{equation}
each with connected fibres. When $b \ge 2$, these fibrations are non-isotrivial, i.e. the fibres of $f_i$ vary in moduli; in the terminology of \cite{CatRol09}, the surface $S$ is therefore a \emph{double Kodaira surface}, equipped with the two distinct Kodaira fibrations $f_1$ and $f_2$.

Moreover, all the topological and biregular invariants of the surface $S$ can be recovered from the admissible epimorphism $\varphi \colon \mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b) \longrightarrow G$ used in its construction. For a comprehensive discussion of this correspondence, we refer the reader to the works cited above, whereas for further information about double Kodaira surfaces  one can see \cite{Rol10, Cat17, LLR17} and the references contained therein.

The previous discussion shows that it makes sense to classify admissible quotients $G$ of    $\mathsf{P}_2(\Sigma_b)$. In the case $b=2$, this classification was carried out in \cite{PolSab22} for admissible quotients of order at most $32$, and subsequently extended in \cite{PolSab24} for admissible quotients of order up to $64$. 

In this paper, which may be viewed as a continuation of these works, we provide a complete classification of admissible quotients $G$ with $|G| \leq 127$, for every genus $b$. Our main result is the following, see Theorem \ref{thm:main}.

