\end{theorem}

post_theorem_intro_text:
The symbol $\sim $ means that the ratio of the quantities tends to $1$.
A fact of particular note is that the expression $ \rho_t\cdot \kappa_d/2^d$ appearing in the last two regimes is exactly the expected number of points in an arbitrary ball of radius $r_t/2$ within $W$; clearly, the number of points in any such fixed ball is a lower bound for the dimension (in either complex). It is quite natural that $D/\rho_t\to \infty$ would holds with high probability; however, somewhat surprisingly, in the denser regimes the dimension and the expected number of points in a fixed ball are of the same order of magnitude, and in fact asymptotically the same if $\rho_t / \ln t\to \infty$. 

We also mention that for a wide range of functionals, a shift in behaviour occurs when the limit of $\rho_t$ changes from zero to a nonzero constant to infinity. In our case, however, the shifts happen for different asymptotics of $\rho_t$ as seen in the theorem.

In the setting where the vertices arise from a binomial point process $X_n$ with parameter $n$ (as opposed to a Poisson point process), the clique number of $\mathcal G(X_n,r_n)$ was investigated in \cites{M08,MM11}, the scan statistic in $\mathbb{R}^2$ in \cite{M99}, as well as a more general set of results in \cite{P02}.
In particular, \cites{M08,M99,P02} give two-point concentration results in the sparser regimes, while in \cite{MM11} an almost sure convergence is shown in all regimes, with the same asymptotics as in our results. Kahle et al. \cite{KTW24} considered similar questions in perturbed (binomial) random geometric graphs.

Our next result concerns large deviations, whose definition we shortly recall here (for more details see \cite{LDPbook}).
A family $(\PP_t)_{t>0}$ of probability measures on a topological space $\mathcal X$ with $\sigma$-field $\mathcal B$ is said to satisfy a large deviations principle (LDP) with speed $s_t\to \infty$ and good rate function $I(\cdot)$ if the levels sets $\{x:I(x)\leq \alpha\}$ are compact for all $\alpha\in[0,\infty)$, and for all $B\in \mathcal B$
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LDP}
    -\inf_{x\in \inter B} I(x) \leq \liminf_{t\to \infty} s_t^{-1} \log \PP_t(B) \leq \limsup_{t\to\infty} s_t^{-1} \log \PP_t(B)\leq - \inf_{x\in {\rm cl }\, B} I(x)
\end{equation}
holds, where $\inter(\cdot)$ and ${\rm cl }\,(\cdot)$ stand for the interior and closure operator, respectively. Further, we say that a family $(X_t)_{t>0}$ of random variables satisfies an LDP if their distributions do.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:LDP}
Let $\eta_t$, $\rho_t$ and $D$ be as in \Cref{thm:main}, and assume $\rho_t/t^{-\alpha}\to \infty$ for all $\alpha>0$. Then $D/n_t$ satisfies an LDP with speed $m_t$ and good rate function $I(x)$, where
\begin{align*}
\intertext{i) for $\rho_t/\ln t\to 0$:}
n_t & = \displaystyle \frac{\ln t}{\ln (t / \rho_t)},&  m_t &= \ln t, & \text{ and } && I(x) &= \begin{cases} x-1 & \text{ if } x\geq 1, \\ \infty & \text{ otherwise;}\end{cases} \\
\intertext{ii) for $\rho_t/\ln t\to B\in (0,\infty)$:}
n_t & = \frac{\kappa_d}{2^d} \rho_t & m_t & = \frac{\kappa_d}{2^d} \rho_t, & \text{ and } && I(x) &= \begin{cases} 1+x\ln x-x-\frac 1B & \text{ if } x\geq \beta, \\ \infty & \text{ otherwise;}\end{cases}\\
\intertext{iii) for $\rho_t / \ln t\to \infty$:}
n_t & = \frac{\kappa_d}{2^d} \rho_t, & m_t &= \frac{\kappa_d}{2^d} \rho_t,& \text{ and } && I(x) &= \begin{cases} 1+x\ln x-x & \text{ if } x\geq 1, \\ \infty & \text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}\\ 
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}

To the author's knowledge, there are no precise distribution results known about the dimension in the $\rho_t /\ln t\to \infty$ regime. This is the topic of the following result for $d=1$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:Gumbel}
    Let $\eta_t$ be a homogeneous Poisson point process on $W=[0,1]\subset \mathbb{R}$, and let $\rho_t / \ln t\to \infty$. Then $D$ satisfies a Gumbel limit theorem; more precisely, 
    $$\mathbb{P}\left(\frac{D-a_t}{b_t} \leq x\right) \to e^{-e^{-x}} \text{ as } t \to \infty,$$ 
    where 
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:standardise}
    \begin{gathered}
        a_t =  \rho_t \left( 1+ \sqrt{\frac{2\ln(t/\rho_t)}{\rho_t}} \cdot \left(1+\frac{\ln(\ln(t/\rho_t))}{4\ln (t/\rho_t)} - \frac{\ln \sqrt{\pi}}{2\ln(t/\rho_t)}\right)\right) \\
        \text{ and } b_t = \sqrt{\frac{\rho_t}{2\ln(t/\rho_t)}}.
    \end{gathered}
    \end{equation}
\end{theorem}

This is obtained as a consequence of the following.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:Poisson}
    Let $\eta_t$ be a homogeneous Poisson point process on $W=[0,1]\subset \mathbb{R}$, and let $\rho_t / \ln t\to \infty$.
    Let $I_n:=[nr_t,(n+1)r_t)\cap [0,1]$ for $n=1,\ldots, N := \lfloor 1/r_t \rfloor$, and for arbitrary $k\in \mathbb{N}$, denote by $X_n^{(k)}$ the indicator of the event that $I_n\cup I_{n+1}$ contains an interval of length $r_t$ with at least $k$ points. Define $X^{(k)}:= \sum_{n=1}^N X_n^{(k)}$, and let $Z^{(k)}\sim \textnormal{Po}(\mathbb{E} X^{(k)})$. Then, if $k=k_t = a_t+x\cdot b_t$ as defined in (\ref{eq:standardise}), then $E X^{(k)}\to e^{-x}$, and
    $$\dd_{TV}(X^{(k)},Z^{(k)})= O\left(\frac{1}{\ln(1/r_t)}\right),$$
    where $\dd_{TV}$ denotes the total variation distance.
    In particular, convergence in distribution to a Poisson random variable with parameter $e^{-x}$ follows.
\end{theorem}

Clearly, we have that $D\geq k-1 \Leftrightarrow X^{(k)}\geq 1$, and \Cref{thm:Gumbel} automatically follows.

We suspect that a similar result should hold in $\mathbb{R}^d$ for arbitrary $d>1$ as well, with the intervals being replaced by cubes, and as a consequence, a limit theorem for the dimension in the $l_{\infty}$-norm; however, getting such a precise result on the distribution appears to be difficult, the question of which will be discussed in the proof. Note also that this result quantifies how much larger the dimension is compared to the lower bound of the number of points in a fixed ball: we get an additional logarithmic factor in the second-order term.

The paper is structured as follows. In \Cref{section:prelim}, we define some of the necessary objects in more detail, and recall some well-known results and computational tools. The proof of Theorems~\ref{thm:main} and \ref{thm:LDP} are split between Sections~\ref{section:exp} and \ref{section:tpc}: the former contains bounds on the tails in the regimes with $\rho_t$ asymptotically larger than all $t^{-\alpha}$ ($\alpha>0$), which are then used to prove the convergence in probability, expectation, and LDP; the latter section discusses the two-point concentration result, and the expectation in the $\rho_t = O(t^{-\alpha})$ for some $\alpha>0$ regime. Lastly, \Cref{section:dense} contains the proof of \Cref{thm:Poisson}.

