full_context:
A group $G$ is said to be residually finite if the intersection of all finite index normal subgroups is trivial. Hence, for every non-trivial element $g\in G$, there exists a finite index normal subgroup $N_g\lhd_f G$ such that $g\notin N_g$. Equivalently, there exists a homomorphism $\varphi_g: G \to Q_g$ to a finite group such that $\varphi_g(g) \neq e$ by taking $Q_g = G/N_g$. The residual finiteness growth $\RF_G: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is a function that quantifies this property for finitely generated residually finite groups. If $\|\cdot\|_G$ denotes a word norm on $G$, then $\RF_G$ is the smallest function such that for every $g\in G$ with $0\neq \|g\|_G \leq n$ there exists $N_g \lhd_f G$ with $g\notin N_g$ and $[G:N_g] \leq \RF_G(n)$.

The study of the residual finiteness growth started in the founding paper \cite{bou2010quantifying}. Since then, this function has been studied for various classes, including free groups \cite{bradford2019short}, linear groups \cite{franz2017quantifying}, virtually abelian groups \cite{math_virt_ab}, virtually solvable minimax groups \cite{dere2025residual}, and certain branch groups \cite{branch_groups}. More information concerning the current state of this field can be found in the survey article \cite{survey2022}. This paper focuses on the class of finitely generated nilpotent groups. Their residual finiteness growth was first studied in \cite{bou2010quantifying}, where it was shown that $\RF_G \preceq \log^{h(G)}$ with $h(G)$ is the Hirsch length of $G$. Since then, better polylogarithmic bounds have been established in \cite{dere2019corrigendum} and more recently in \cite{dere2025residual}. Nonetheless, many questions remain unanswered, including the exact behavior of $\RF_G$, and in this paper, we address several of these open questions.

Firstly, it was unknown how $\RF_G$ behaves when taking a finite index subgroup when $G$ is nilpotent. In general, it is known that commensurable groups do not necessarily have the same residual finiteness growth, as illustrated for example in \cite{MR2925403,math_virt_ab}. However, for nilpotent groups it was shown that $\RF_G = \RF_{G/T(G)}$ in \cite{MarkPubl}, where $T(G)$ denotes the torsion subgroup of $G$, indicating that commensurable nilpotent groups could have the same residual finiteness growth. Our first main result confirms this intuition:

Let $G\leq (\mathfrak{u}^0(n, \Q), \ast)$ be an LR-group with underlying Lie ring structure $L$. Both $G$ and $L$ have a metric structure, namely as a finitely generated group and as a $\Z$-module, which we introduce below, including the relation between both.
 \begin{df}
 Let $G$ be a finitely generated group with finite generating set $S$. The \emph{word norm} on $G$ via $S$ is defined as 
 $$\norm{g}_{G,S} = \min\{k \mid g = s_1\dots s_k, s_i\in S\cup S^{-1}, k\in \N\cup\{0\}\}.$$
 \end{df} 
 Since $L^\Q$ as a $\Q$-vector space, we can take a norm $\norm{\cdot}_L$ on $L$ by restricting a norm on $L^\Q$. Below, we define the Guivarc'h length of a nilpotent Lie algebra or Lie ring. This length function first appeared in the proof of \cite[Theorem II.1]{MR369608} in order to obtain improved bounds on the word growth of nilpotent groups.
 \begin{df}
 Let $\mathfrak{g}$ denote a finite-dimensional nilpotent Lie $\R$-algebra of nilpotency class $c$ with lower central series $\left(\gamma_i(\mathfrak{g})\right)_{1\leq i\leq c}$. Take vector spaces $\mathfrak{a}_i \subset \mathfrak{g}$ such that $\gamma_i(\mathfrak{g}) = \mathfrak{a}_i \oplus \ldots \oplus \mathfrak{a}_c$ for all $1\leq i \leq c$. Choose a vector space norm $\norm{\cdot}_i$ on $\mathfrak{a}_i$. A \emph{Guivarc'h length} $l_G$ with respect to the chosen decomposition and norms for a vector $v\in \mathfrak{g}$ is given by
 $$l_G(v) := \sup\{(\norm{v_i}_i)^{1/i}\mid v = v_1 + \ldots + v_c \in \mathfrak{a}_1 \oplus \ldots \oplus \mathfrak{a}_c\}.$$
 \end{df}
 \begin{df}
 A Guivarc'h length $l_G$ on a (nilpotent) Lie ring $L$ is defined as the restriction of a Guivarc'h length on $L^\R$ to $L$. 
 \end{df}
 The Guivarc'h length depends on the choice of decomposition $\mathfrak{a}_i$ and the choice of norms $\norm{\cdot}_i$ on each component. However, if $l'_G$ is another Guivarc'h length on the Lie ring $L$, then by \cite{MR369608} there exists a constant $C>0$ such that
 \begin{equation} \label{eq_equiv_Guiv}
 \dfrac{1}{C}l'_G(v) \leq l_G(v) \leq Cl'_G(v).
 \end{equation}
 As $\max\left\{\norm{v_i}_i\mid v = v_1 + \ldots + v_c \in \mathfrak{a}_1 \oplus \ldots \oplus \mathfrak{a}_c\right\}$ defines a genuine norm on $\mathfrak{g}$. and all norms on finite-dimensional vector spaces are equivalent, there exists for every norm $\norm{\cdot}$ on a Lie ring $L$ a constant $C > 0$ such that
 \begin{equation}\label{eq_norm_to_Gui}
 \dfrac{1}{C}\norm{v} \leq l_G(v)^c \leq C\norm{v}^c.
 \end{equation}

In an LR-group, word norms are equivalent with Guivarc'h lengths on the underlying Lie ring structure, as is shown in \cite{MR369608}:
 \begin{prop} \label{prop_Guivarch}
 Let $G\leq (\mathfrak{u}^0(n, \Q), \ast)$ be an LR-group with underlying Lie ring structure $L$. Let $l_G$ denote a Guivarc'h length on $L$ and fix a generating set of $G$ with induced word norm $\norm{\cdot}_G$. Then, there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for all $g\in G$
 $$\dfrac{1}{C}\norm{g}_G \leq l_G(g) \leq C\norm{g}_G .$$
 \end{prop}
 \subsection{Residual Finiteness Growth} \label{sec_RF}
 In this subsection, we will introduce that residual finiteness growth of finitely generated groups and of Lie rings. The notion for groups originally appeared in \cite{bou2010quantifying} and has produced a large number of results as mentioned in the introduction. The notion for Lie rings is however new, although other generalization of residual finiteness growth to certain algebraic structures exist, see for example \cite{bou2010quantifying,franz2017quantifying}, where residual finiteness growth is defined for rings of integers over number fields and finitely generated integral domains respectively.

\section{Commensurable nilpotent groups} \label{sec_invariance}
 In this section, we will prove Theorem \ref{thm_intro_commens}, namely that commensurable finitely generated nilpotent groups have the same residual finiteness growth. Let us start by recalling the notion of commensurability.
 \begin{df}
 Two groups $G_1$ and $G_2$ are called \emph{commensurable} if there exist subgroups $H_1 \leq_f G_1$ and $H_2 \leq_f G_2$ such that $H_1\cong H_2$ are isomorphic.
 \end{df}
 We start by showing the result for $\mathcal{I}$-groups:
 \begin{thm} \label{thm_Q_inv}
 Let $G_1$ and $G_2$ be two commensurable $\mathcal{I}$-groups, then $\RF_{G_1} = \RF_{G_2}$.
 \end{thm}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem_baumslag}
 Let $G$ be a finitely generated nilpotent group with torsion subgroup $T$. There exists an embedding $i: G \to G/T\times Q$ for some finite group $Q$ such that $i(G) \leq_f G/T\times Q$.
 \end{lemma}
 \begin{proof}
 Since $T$ is finite and $G$ is residually finite, we can find a homomorphism $\psi: G \to Q$ to a finite group $Q$, such that $\psi(t) \neq e$ for all non-trivial $t\in T$. Now, the map $i$ can be taken as
 $$i: G \to G/T \times Q: g \mapsto (gT, \psi(g)).$$
 If $\pi$ denotes the projection of $G/T\times Q$ onto $G/T$, then $\pi(i(G)) = G/T$, so
 $$[G/T\times Q: i(G)] = [G/T: \pi(i(G))]\cdot [Q: Q\cap i(G)] \leq 1 \cdot |Q| < \infty. $$
 \end{proof}
 \begin{thm}
 Let $G_1$ and $G_2$ denote two finitely generated nilpotent groups. If $G_1$ and $G_2$ are commensurable, then $\RF_{G_1} = \RF_{G_2}$.
 \end{thm}
 \begin{proof}
 Just as in Theorem \ref{thm_Q_inv}, it suffices to show the claim for the case where $G_1 \leq_f G_2$. Let $T$ denote the torsion subgroup of $G_2$. Consider the embedding $i: G_2 \to G_2/T\times Q$ of Lemma \ref{lem_baumslag}. Identify $G_2$ with its embedding $i(G_2)$ in $G_2/T\times Q$. Now, we have inclusions
 $$G_1 \leq_f G_2 \leq_f G_2/T\times Q.$$
 The intersection $G = G_1\cap (G_2/T\times\{e\})$ is torsion-free as a subgroup of the torsion-free group $G_2/T$, and clearly of finite index in $G_1$.

Hence, $G$ is also a finite index subgroup of $G_2/T\times\{e\}$.
 In particular, Theorem \ref{thm_Q_inv} states that $\RF_G = \RF_{G_2/T}$. By all the inclusions above, we have that
 $$\RF_G \preceq \RF_{G_1} \preceq \RF_{G_2} \preceq \RF_{G_2/T\times Q} = \max\{\RF_{G_2/T}, \RF_{Q}\} = \RF_{G_2/T}.$$
 Thus $\RF_{G_1} = \RF_{G_2}$ follows immediately from $\RF_G = \RF_{G_2/T}$.
 \end{proof}
 By Lemma \ref{lem_baumslag} and Lemma \ref{lem_LR_inside}, we have the following two immediate consequences, where the first thus gives an alternative proof for the one provided in \cite{MarkPubl}.
 \begin{cor}
 Let $G$ be a finitely generated nilpotent group with torsion subgroup $T$, then $\RF_G = \RF_{G/T}$.
 \end{cor}
 \begin{cor} \label{cor_from_I_to_LR}
 Let $G_1$ be a finitely generated nilpotent group, then there exists an LR-group $G_2$ such that $\RF_{G_1} = \RF_{G_2}$.
 \end{cor}
 \section{Residual finiteness growth of nilpotent Lie rings} \label{sec_corresp}
Corollary \ref{cor_from_I_to_LR} shows that for every finitely generated nilpotent group $G_1$, there exists an LR-group $G_2$ such that $\RF_{G_1} = \RF_{G_2}$. Hence, it suffices to find estimates for $\RF_G$ of LR-groups $G\leq (\mathfrak{u}^0(n, \Q), \ast)$. In this section, we will demonstrate that $\RF_G$ equals $\RF_L^{\Gui}$, where $L$ denotes the underlying Lie ring structure of $G$.

post_theorem_intro_text:
Secondly, torsion-free nilpotent groups $G$ are well known for their strong relation to Lie algebras via the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff formula. Theorem \ref{thm_intro_commens} can be rephrased by stating that $RF_G$ is an invariant of the rational Mal'cev completion of $G$, and thus of its corresponding rational Lie algebra. The interplay between the group and the algebra setting has been heavily exploited in \cite{dere2025residual} to derive upper bounds. So far, there is no result consolidating this relation, and our second main theorem establishes a precise correspondence between nilpotent groups and Lie rings:
	\begin{thmx} \label{thm_intro_corresp}
		If $G$ is a finitely generated nilpotent group, then there exists a nilpotent Lie ring $L$ of finite rank such that $\RF_G = \RF_L$ with respect to a Guivarc'h length on $L$. Conversely, if $L$ is a nilpotent Lie ring of finite rank, there exists a finitely generated nilpotent group $G$ such that $\RF_L = \RF_G$ with respect to a Guivarc'h length on $L$.
	\end{thmx}
\noindent Here, $\RF_L$ is defined using a Guivarc'h length on the Lie ring $L$ and by using ideals (instead of normal subgroups) in $L$. 

 Theorem \ref{thm_intro_commens} is a necessary ingredient to obtain this result. Indeed, a finitely generated torsion-free nilpotent group $\tilde{G}$ can be embedded via upper triangular, unipotent matrices in $\GL(m, \mathbb{Z})$ for some $m\in \mathbb{N}$, and the logarithm maps $\tilde{G}$ into a rational algebra $\mathfrak{g}$. We show that if this image is a Lie ring $L$, then $\RF_{\tilde{G}} = \RF_L$. We conclude by noting that any nilpotent group $G$ is commensurable with a group as above. In particular, the assignment of $L$ to $G$ is not unique, as any choice of Lie ring $L\subset \mathfrak{g}$ such that $L\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathbb{Q} = \mathfrak{g}$ will satisfy $\RF_G = \RF_L$.

	Thirdly, it remains unclear whether the upper bound of the form $\log^\delta$ recently given in \cite{dere2025residual} is optimal, where $\delta$ will be formally introduced in Section \ref{sec_upper}. Exactness has for example been established for two-step nilpotent groups with one or two-dimensional commutator subgroups, see \cite{dere2025twostep}. Interestingly, the value $\delta$ is an invariant of the complex Mal'cev completion of the nilpotent group $G$, and it is conjectured that $\RF_G$ itself is an invariant of this completion. Related to this question, it is also still open whether $\RF_G$ is a quasi-isometric invariant of $G$ within the class of nilpotent groups. 

	In this paper, we use the corresponding Lie setting of Theorem \ref{thm_intro_corresp} to illustrate that the bound $\log^\delta$ is optimal in a sense, as we explain now. Let $\mathcal{P}$ denote a subset of normal subgroups. We can define $\RF_{G, \mathcal{P}}$ as the smallest function such that for every $g\in G$ with $0\neq \|g\|_G \leq n$ there exists $N_g \in \mathcal{P}$ with $g\notin N_g$ and $[G:N_g] \leq \RF_{G, \mathcal{P}}(n)$, see Section \ref{sec_RF}. If $\mathcal{P}$ is the set of all normal subgroup, then we recover the definition of the residual finiteness growth. Clearly, $\RF_G(n) \leq \RF_{G, \mathcal{P}}(n)$ for any choice of $\mathcal{P}$. We show that
	\begin{thmx} \label{thm_intro_via_primes}
		Let $G$ be a torsion-free nilpotent group and $\mathcal{P}$ the subset of normal subgroups $N$ such that $G^p \subset N \subset G$ for some prime number $p$. Then, $\RF_{G, \mathcal{P}} = \log^\delta$ with $\delta$ the constant of the upper bound in \cite{dere2025residual}.
	\end{thmx}
	\noindent 	As a direct consequence of Theorem \ref{thm_intro_via_primes}, we also see that $\RF_G \preceq \log^\delta \preceq \log^{\psi(G)}$, where $\log^{\psi(G)}$ is the bound given in \cite{dere2019corrigendum}. 

	This result and the exact functions for nilpotent groups known in the literature lead to the conjecture that the bound of \cite{dere2025residual} is in fact exact.
\begin{conj} \label{conj}
	For every torsion-free nilpotent group $G$ it holds that $\RF_G = \log^\delta$.
\end{conj}

	The structure of this article is as follows. The preliminaries presented in Section \ref{sec_prelim} introduce the correspondence between Lie group and Lie algebra, specifically in the nilpotent case, and simultaneously the notion of residual finiteness growth for groups and Lie rings. In order to show that $\RF_G = \RF_L$ of Theorem \ref{thm_intro_corresp} in Section \ref{sec_corresp}, we first show that $\RF_G$ is a commensurability invariant for nilpotent groups in Section \ref{sec_invariance}. Finally, we proceed to prove Theorem \ref{thm_intro_via_primes} in Section \ref{sec_upper}, which is the largest part of the paper.

