ref_dict.ex:curves_isomorphic_after_base_change:
\begin{example}
    \label{ex:curves_isomorphic_after_base_change}
    There is a pair of non-isomorphic elliptic curves $E_1$ and $E_2$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ that are Fourier--Mukai partners (see \cite[Example 2.3.11]{Ward:2014}). From \cite[Theorem 2.2]{Orlov:2002}, it follows that any triangulated equivalence $D^b_{\operatorname{coh}}(E_1) \to D^b_{\operatorname{coh}}(E_2)$ is given by an integral transform. 
    Note that an elliptic curve is geometrically integral and projective. 
    By \Cref{prop:curves_isomorphic_after_base_change}, there is a finite field extension $L/\mathbb{Q}$ such that $E_1 \times_{\mathbb{Q}} \operatorname{Spec}(L) \cong E_2 \times_{\mathbb{Q}} \operatorname{Spec}(L)$.
\end{example}

