The \emph{Alperin weight (AW)} conjecture, proposed by Alperin in 1987 \cite{Al87}, has been a central problem in the modular representation theory. Together with the earlier \emph{McKay conjecture} (formulated by McKay in 1971 \cite{Mc71,Mc72}), it belongs to the class of \emph{global–local counting conjectures}, which relate the representation theory of a finite group to that of its local subgroups.
The work of Navarro and Tiep \cite{NT11} marked a breakthrough by \emph{reducing} the AW conjecture to simple groups. They proved that the conjecture holds provided every finite non-abelian simple group satisfies the so-called \emph{inductive AW condition} (referred to as the AWC-good condition in \cite{NT11}), a requirement substantially stronger than the original conjecture.
In 2013, Sp\"ath \cite{Spa13} also achieved a reduction for the \emph{blockwise Alperin weight (BAW)} conjecture.
For progress on the inductive investigation of the Alperin weight conjecture, we refer to the survey paper \cite{FZ22}.

