Let $G\subset \mathrm{GL}(\mathrm V)$ be the connected component of a classical group over a finite field, attached to a space $(\mathrm V,\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle)$, and let $\chi:Z(\mathrm{GL}(\mathrm V))\to \mathbb C^\times$ be a multiplicative character, where $Z(\mathrm{GL}(\mathrm V))$ denotes the scalar matrices (assume $\chi$ is not conjugate-dual). Define the kernel function on $G$ by
\[
K_{\mathrm{V},\chi}(g)=\begin{cases}
\chi\bigl(\det(\mathrm{Id}_{\mathrm{V}}+g)\bigr),& \det(\mathrm{Id}_{\mathrm{V}}+g)\neq 0,\\
0,& \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
A function on $G$ is called stable if it is constant on stable conjugacy classes. Under these assumptions, which statement about $K_{\mathrm{V},\chi}$ holds?

theorem.content:
\label{thm:main}
Let $M,N\in\mathbb{N}$, with $\lcm(M,N)$ not
divisible by $2^4$ or the square of an odd prime.
Let $f\in S_k^{\rm new}(\Gamma_0(M))$ and $g\in S_\ell^{\rm new}(\Gamma_0(N))$
be non-zero cusp forms with real Fourier coefficients
$a_f(n),a_g(n)$. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $a_f(n)a_g(n)\ge0$ for all sufficiently large $n\in\mathbb{N}$;
\item $(M,k)=(N,\ell)$ and $f/g$ is a positive constant.
\end{enumerate}
Thus if $f$ and $g$ are not proportional then
$\{n\in\mathbb{N}:\epsilon a_f(n)a_g(n)>0\}$
is infinite for each $\epsilon\in\{\pm1\}$.

