A proof strategy for Theorem~\ref{thm: stratification of MHACS} is outlined as follows:

- The authors state that the “main tool is the robust approach of Naber-Valtorta \cite{Naber-V-2017},” and that they aim to “apply their method” to minimizing harmonic almost complex structures.

- The main technical difficulty in adapting this method is the monotonicity formula. We first record the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity formula]\label{thm: monotonicity formula}
			{Let $J\in W^{1,2}(\mathcal{J}_g(B_1(p)))$ be a minimizing harmonic almost complex structure on $B_1(p)\subset M$.} Then, for any $a\in B_{\frac{1}{2}}(p)$ and every $0 < r< R \\le\frac12$, there holds
			\begin{equation}\label{eq: monotonicity formula}
				e^{C_0r}r^{2-m}\int_{B_r(a)}|D J|^2dx+\int_{B_R\backslash B_r(a)}\rho^{2-m}|\partial_\rho J|^2dx\le e^{C_0R}R^{2-m}\int_{B_R(a)}|D J|^2dx+C_0(R-r).
			\end{equation}
			where $C_0>0$ is a constant, $\rho=|x-a|$ and $\partial_{\rho}J=DJ\cdot\frac{x-a}{|x-a|}$.
	\end{theorem}

In place of a monotonicity equality, this provides a “monotonicity inequality with an extra error term” (MI). Consequently, they “have to thoroughly check the whole procedure of \cite{Naber-V-2017}, where a monotonicity equality was used,” and they conclude that “this inequality is sufficient to do the quantitative regularity analysis as that of \cite{Naber-V-2017}.”

- They identify further “important ingredients of the argument” needed for the adaptation: an “important compactness property and unique continuation property for minimizing harmonic almost complex structures,” and they “explore some basic properties of tangent almost complex structures.” These are said to “play a fundamental role in adapting the quantitative $\varepsilon$-regularity theorem of minimizing harmonic maps to minimizing harmonic almost complex structures.”

- The structure of the proof is organized into components: they (i) “establish the partial regularity theory, compactness theory and unique continuation property”; (ii) “develop the quantitative $\varepsilon$-regularity theorem” using those results; (iii) “recall the rectifiable Reifenberg theorems from \cite{Naber-V-2017}”; (iv) “adapt the Naber-Valtorta covering argument to minimizing harmonic almost complex structures”; and (v) “prove our main results,” thereby establishing the main theorem.

