The function of two variables, \begin{align*}
    f\left(a,b\right)=\frac{\gcd\left(a+b, ab\right)}{\gcd\left(a, b\right)}
\end{align*}
is of interest in this paper. At a basic level, $f$ packages the two fundamental operations $+$ and $\cdot$ into a single invariant: it measures the extent to which the additive combination $a+b$ shares common divisors with the multiplicative combination $ab$. In other words, $f\left(a,b\right)$ investigates the common divisibility forced by the interaction between addition and multiplication.

We claim the function \[f:\mathbb{N}\times \mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}\quad\text{where}\quad \left(a,b\right)\mapsto \frac{\gcd\left(a+b, ab\right)}{\gcd\left(a, b\right)}\]
is surjective and that every natural number (in the codomain) can be attained (Propositions \ref{proposition f(a,b) is an integer} and \ref{proposition f is a surjection}).

