From our earlier discussion, when studying the map $f\left(i,j\right)$, we now infer that $\left(i,j\right)$ behaves like a pair whose trace $i+j$ and determinant $ij$ form a primitive polynomial $x^2-\left(i+j\right)x+ij$ modulo $p$. As such, the map \[\left(i,j\right)\mapsto \left(t,d\right)=\left(i+j,ij\right)\]
is precisely the same arithmetic correspondence that classifies companion matrices \[\begin{bmatrix}
 0 & -d \\ 1 & t
\end{bmatrix}\in \operatorname{GL}\left(2,\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}\right).\]
Each conjugacy class in $\operatorname{GL}\left(2,\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}\right)$ corresponds, up to a distinct primitive pair $\left(t,d\right)$ modulo $n$. As such, the density $\rho_n$ measures how often randomly chosen integers $\left(i,j\right)$ produce a primitive pair $\left(t,d\right)$, which is equivalent to the normalised of distinct characteristic polynomials among all $2\times 2$ invertible matrices modulo $n$. Hence, \[\rho_n\sim \frac{\text{number of conjugacy classes in }\operatorname{GL}\left(2,\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}\right)}{n^2}\]
but convergence is slower in contrast to our initial gcd map.
\section{$f_r(a,b)$ for $r\ge 2$}
A natural way to probe how much of the behaviour of $f\left(a,b\right)$ is genuinely tied to the linear additive structure \(a+b\) is to replace \(a+b\) with a higher-degree sum \(a^r+b^r\). Accordingly, for $r\in\mathbb{N}$, we define
Let \begin{align}\label{eqn: f_r(a,b)}
f_r:\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}\quad\text{where}\quad \left(a,b\right)\mapsto\frac{\operatorname{gcd}\left(a^r+b^r,ab\right)}{\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)}.
\end{align}
In particular, $f_1\left(a,b\right)=f\left(a,b\right)$. For our discussion, let $r\ge 2$. Note that $f_r\left(a,b\right)=1$ if and only if $\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)=1$. Consequently, the limiting density of pairs $\left(a,b\right)\in \mathbb{N}^2$ for which $f_r(a,b)=1$ is the classical coprimality probability
\begin{align}\label{eq:rho_r_ge_2}
\rho
=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}\left|\left\{\left(a,b\right)\in\left[1,n\right]\times\left[1,n\right]: f_r(a,b)=1\right\}\right|
=\frac{1}{\zeta(2)}
=\prod_{p}\left(1-\frac{1}{p^2}\right)
=\frac{6}{\pi^2}.
\end{align}
The significance of \eqref{eq:rho_r_ge_2} is as follows. For $r\ge 2$, the normalised quantity $f_r(a,b)$ collapses to $\gcd(a,b)$ itself. In other words, once $r\ge 2$, the expression (\ref{eqn: f_r(a,b)}) no longer contains any subtle interaction between the additive structure $a^r+b^r$ and the multiplicative structure $ab$. Therefore, the event $f_r\left(a,b\right)=1$ is exactly the coprimality event $\gcd\left(a,b\right)=1$, and its
density must match the classical probability that two random integers are relatively prime. Equivalently, the Euler product in \eqref{eq:rho_r_ge_2} reflects independence across primes: for each prime $p$, the proportion of pairs $\left(a,b\right)$ with $p\mid \gcd(a,b)$ is $1/p^2$, so the proportion with $p\nmid \gcd(a,b)$ is $1-1/p^2$; multiplying over $p$ yields $1/\zeta(2)$.

To make \eqref{eq:rho_r_ge_2} quantitative, one can count coprime pairs in $[1,n]\times [1,n]$ and eventually make use of Theorem \ref{theorem sum of totient}. We first introduce the Dirichlet hyperbola method (Theorem \ref{thm: Dirichlet hyperbola method}), which we would see is a powerful technique in estimating the sum of arithmetic functions \cite{Apostol}.
\begin{theorem}[Dirichlet hyperbola method]\label{thm: Dirichlet hyperbola method}
Let $f$ be a multiplicative function. Define \begin{align*}
 F\left(n\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}f\left(k\right).
\end{align*}
Let $f=g\ast h$, where $\ast$ denotes Dirichlet convolution and $g$ and $h$ are multiplicative functions, so the sum becomes \begin{align}\label{eq: dirichlet hyperbola split up sum}
 F\left(n\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{xy=k}g\left(x\right)h\left(y\right).
\end{align} Then, \begin{align}\label{eq: dirichlet hypetbola final formula}
F\left(n\right)=\sum _{x=1}^{a}\sum _{y=1}^{n/x}g(x)h(y)+\sum _{y=1}^{b}\sum _{x=1}^{n/y}g(x)h(y)-\sum _{x=1}^{a}\sum _{y=1}^{b}g(x)h(y) 
\end{align}
\end{theorem}
Although technically the inner sum in (\ref{eq: dirichlet hyperbola split up sum}) runs over all ordered pairs $\left(x,y\right)\in\mathbb{N}^2$, we can leave our notation as mentioned. On the $xy$-plane, these pairs $\left(x,y\right)$ lie on a hyperbola, and when the double sum is fully expanded, there is a bijection between the terms of the sum and the lattice points in the first quadrant on the hyperbolas of the form $xy=k$, where $1\le k \le n$ is an integer.
\begin{figure}[H]
 \centering
 \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]

Also, the geometric interpretation of the Dirichlet hyperbola method generally involves the principle of inclusion and inclusion. Let $a\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $1<a<n$, and let $b=n/a$. Then the lattice points $\left(x,y\right)$ can be split into three overlapping regions: one region is bounded by $1\le x\le a$ and $1\le y\le n/x$, another region is bounded by $1\le y\le b$ and $1 \le x \le n/y$, and the third is bounded by $1\le x\le a$ and $1\le y\le b$. In Figure \ref{fig: Geometric interpretation of the Dirichlet hyperbola method}, the first region is the $R_1\cup R_2$, the second region is $R_2\cup R_3$, and the third region is $R_2$. Note that $R_2=R_1\cap R_3$. By the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the sum in (\ref{eq: dirichlet hyperbola split up sum}) is the sum over the first region, plus the sum over the second region, minus the sum over the third region. This yields (\ref{eq: dirichlet hypetbola final formula}). 
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem sum of totient}
Let $\varphi\left(n\right)$ denote the Euler totient function. For $x>1$, we have \begin{align*}
 \sum_{n\le x}\varphi\left(n\right)=\frac{3x^2}{\pi^2}+\mathcal{O}(x^{3/2}).
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Note that $\varphi=\mu\ast N$, where $N\left(n\right)=n$ denotes the identity function. By applying the Dirichlet hyperbola method with $f=N$ and $g=\mu$ (Theorem \ref{thm: Dirichlet hyperbola method}), we have \begin{align*}
 \sum_{n\le x}\varphi\left(n\right)=\sum_{n\le y}\mu\left(n\right)F\left(\frac{x}{n}\right)+\sum_{m\le x/y}N\left(m\right)G\left(\frac{x}{m}\right)-F\left(\frac{x}{y}\right)G\left(y\right),
\end{align*}
where \begin{align*}
 F\left(x\right)=\sum_{n\le x}N\left(n\right)=\frac{x^2}{2}+O\left(x\right)\quad\text{and}\quad G\left(x\right)=\sum_{n\le x}\mu\left(n\right)=O\left(x\right)
\end{align*}
Letting $y=\sqrt{x}$, one can deduce that \begin{align*}
 \sum_{n\le x}\varphi\left(n\right)=\sum_{n\le \sqrt{x}}\frac{\mu\left(n\right)}{n^2}\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^{3/2}).
\end{align*}
By the Möbius inversion formula, we have \begin{align*}
 \zeta\left(2\right)\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\mu(n)}{n^2}=1\quad\text{so}\quad \sum_{n\le \sqrt{x}}\frac{\mu(n)}{n^2}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\mu(n)}{n^2}+O\left(\sum_{n>\sqrt{x}}\frac{1}{n^2}\right)=\frac{6}{\pi^2}+O(x^{-1/2}).
\end{align*}
The result follows.
\end{proof}
Now, let $N\in\mathbb{N}$ and $S$ denote the set of $\left(a,b\right)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ such that $1\le a,b\le N$. Then by Theorem \ref{theorem sum of totient}, the total number of elements in $S$ such that $\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)=1$ is given by \begin{align*}
 1+2\sum_{b\le N}\varphi(b)=\frac{6}{\pi^2}N^2+O(N^{3/2}),
\end{align*}
This shows that the probability that two randomly chosen positive integers are relatively prime is $6/\pi^2$.
